  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03VT">Theorem <span data-tag="03VT">64.31.2</span>.</a> Given an eigenform $f$ with values in $\overline{\mathbf{Q}}_ l$ and eigenvalues $u_ v\in \overline{\mathbf{Z}}_ l^*$ then there exists </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \rho : \pi _1(X)\to \text{GL}_2(E)  \]
</div>
  <p> continuous, absolutely irreducible where $E$ is a finite extension of $\mathbf{Q}_\ell $ contained in $\overline{\mathbf{Q}}_ l$ such that $t_ v = \text{Tr}(\rho (F_ v))$, and $u_ v = q_ v^{-1}\det \left(\rho (F_ v)\right)$ for all places $v$. </p>
